# The M4 Task 2 gate, as a re-runnable script: does a setting the PLAYER
# changed in a menu survive a reload?
#
#   python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/set-chrome \
#        --script-file web/tools/persist-settings-gate.steps
#   node web/tools/drive-firefox.mjs           --out /tmp/set-firefox \
#        --script-file web/tools/persist-settings-gate.steps
#   node docs/evidence/m4-persist-settings/check-settings-transcript.mjs \
#        /tmp/set-chrome/console.log
#   kill %1
#
# --headed is REQUIRED for Chrome. This script presses keys, and headless
# Chrome 152 floods the page with thousands of spurious keydown events per
# real keypress; see the header of web/tools/drive-browser.mjs. Firefox
# headless is fine.
#
# HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM web/tools/persist-gate.steps. That gate proves
# /persist is IndexedDB-backed and that BYTES survive a reload -- and it says
# so explicitly: "Nothing about the game USING the restored settings." It gets
# its write for free, because rScreen.cpp's sr_InitDisplay and
# lowlevel_sr_InitDisplay call st_SaveConfig() unconditionally on every boot.
# This gate is about the thing that was missing: a save caused by the player.
#
# WHICH SETTING, AND WHY THIS ONE
# -------------------------------
# The player's NAME, typed into the "Name:" field of the First Setup menu.
# It is a uMenuItemString bound directly to ePlayer::name, which is the target
# of the tConfItemLine "PLAYER_1" (src/engine/ePlayer.cpp), so it is a real
# configuration item written into user.cfg by tConfItemBase::SaveAll.
#
# It was chosen against three constraints:
#
#   * ITS MENU PATH DOES NOT ALREADY SAVE. Nothing between leaving First Setup
#     and the end of the first-use flow calls st_SaveConfig. This is not an
#     assumption: run this same script against a build with the menu-leave
#     callback compiled out (armagetronad-nomenusave.html, see
#     web/Makefile's client-control target) and the name is simply lost.
#     The RESOLUTION menu was excluded for exactly the opposite reason --
#     lowlevel_sr_InitDisplay saves on every mode change, so a resolution
#     test would pass whether or not this task's work exists.
#   * IT AVOIDS THE VIEWPORT CONFIGURATION SCREEN, which is a latent abort
#     (rViewport.cpp, the tASSERT in rViewport::Select's caller path) reachable
#     from the display settings menu. Nothing here goes near it.
#   * IT IS REACHABLE IN TWO KEYSTROKES FROM A FRESH PROFILE, so the whole gate
#     is two page loads and about a minute, with no gameplay. Reaching the real
#     main menu instead would mean driving the entire first-use tutorial match
#     first (gArmagetron.cpp runs sg_SinglePlayerGame before it returns), which
#     buys nothing this does not already show.
#
# WHAT IS CLAIMED
# ---------------
#   1. Typing in the menu changes the game's memory and NOT the file. Checked
#      directly: after the name is typed and before the menu is left,
#      /persist/var/user.cfg still holds the old value. So the save this gate
#      goes on to observe is caused by the menu EXIT, not by the keystrokes.
#   2. Leaving the menu saves. One [PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave line appears
#      between the two marks that bracket the Escape keypress, and the file
#      afterwards holds the new name.
#   3. The new name reaches IndexedDB, read over a connection this script
#      opens itself, decoding the FILE_DATA record's `contents` -- and read
#      BEFORE ANY UNLOAD EVENT HAS FIRED. That is what makes the durability
#      claim attributable to the menu-leave save rather than to the
#      beforeunload backstop.
#   4. It survives a real location.reload() into a page that has written
#      nothing.
#   5. The game USES it, not merely stores it. Boot 2 deliberately CLOBBERS
#      /persist/var/user.cfg from JavaScript, then leaves a menu; the file the
#      game rewrites from its own memory has the name back. That cannot be
#      satisfied by bytes surviving on disk -- st_LoadConfig has to have
#      parsed the value into the running program.
#
# WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Nothing about the backstop. No check here may depend on
# visibilitychange or beforeunload, and that is verified rather than intended:
# check S9 asserts no [PERSISTBACKSTOP] line exists before the reload, and the
# whole script is run against armagetronad-nobackstop.html -- the same page
# with both handlers disabled -- where it must still PASS.
#
# WHICH SCREEN BOOT 2 LANDS ON IS NOT DETERMINISTIC, AND THIS SCRIPT IS
# WRITTEN SO THAT IT DOES NOT MATTER. This is a measurement, not a worry.
#
# gArmagetron.cpp's welcome() sets st_FirstUse=false only AFTER
# sg_StartupPlayerMenu returns, and what follows is a uMenu::Message -- which
# is not a uMenu::OnEnter and never fires uCallbackMenuLeave. So the flip from
# FIRST_USE 1 to 0 lands in a gap where the menu-leave save cannot see it. In
# a run of this script on the real page the beforeunload backstop DOES see it:
#
#     boot1-after-menu-leave   21955 bytes  FIRST_USE 1  (menu-leave save)
#     [PERSISTSAVE] js-backstop             <- fires on location.reload()
#     boot2-before-play        22763 bytes  FIRST_USE 0  (backstop's save)
#
# The extra ~800 bytes are the keyboard template sg_StartupPlayerMenu applies
# after the menu closes. So on armagetronad.html boot 2 skips first setup and
# opens on the MAIN MENU, and on armagetronad-nobackstop.html it opens on the
# LANGUAGE MENU. Every step after the reload is therefore screen-independent:
# no key but Escape is pressed, and Escape leaves whichever menu is up.
#
# THE SAME MEASUREMENT IS ALSO THIS TASK'S KNOWN LIMITATION, stated plainly:
# a menu whose caller applies the player's choice AFTER the menu closes is not
# covered by a menu-leave save. The one such menu FOUND is First Setup, whose
# Colour / Controls / Connection items are read out of local variables by
# sg_StartupPlayerMenu after firstSetup.Enter() returns. Its Name field -- the
# one this gate uses -- binds straight to ePlayer::name and is covered.
#
# "The one found", not "the only one": nobody has swept every uMenu in the
# tree. Every menu looked at while building this binds directly, and direct
# binding is what uMenu.h's item classes impose (each holds a `T *target`), so
# further exceptions should be rare -- but that is an expectation, not a
# survey, and it is written this way so the next person can run the sweep
# rather than inherit an unearned universal.
#
# THE TRANSCRIPT IS CUMULATIVE and covers two page loads. The mark
# "RELOAD-REQUESTED" is the partition. Every payload also carries its own
# "phase" string, so the checker never infers which boot a line came from by
# counting. Note that the n= counter in [PERSISTSAVE] restarts at 1 in boot 2
# (it is a static in the wasm, and boot 2 is a new module) -- the checker
# counts LINES, and so do the until: steps below.
#
# THE HELPER INSTALL IS PER-BOOT. window.__set is wiped by the reload, so it
# is installed twice. If either install were skipped the [SETFS] lines for
# that boot would simply not appear and the checker would report them missing.

# ===========================================================================
# BOOT 1
# ===========================================================================

until:1:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
mark:BOOT-1-POPULATED
wait:1000
shot:00-boot1-ready

eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.h=(s)=>{if(s===null)return null;let x=5381;for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++)x=((x*33)^s.charCodeAt(i))>>>0;return x.toString(16)};S.pick=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>l.trim().indexOf(k+' ')===0);return m?m.trim().slice(k.length+1).trim():null};S.fs=(phase)=>{const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');console.log('[SETFS] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,present:c!==null,bytes:c===null?null:c.length,hash:S.h(c),player_1:S.pick(c,'PLAYER_1'),first_use:S.pick(c,'FIRST_USE'),clobbered:c!==null&&c.indexOf('CLOBBERED-BY-THE-GATE')>=0}));return phase};window.__set=S;return 'helpers installed'})()

# A fresh temp browser profile per run, so IndexedDB starts empty and there is
# no user.cfg at all. This is what makes "the name is not in the file yet"
# later on a fact about this run rather than about a leftover profile.
eval:window.__set.fs('boot1-before-play')

# libidbfs.js's own persist state machine, armed before the Play click so the
# game's boot save is seen. `tag` is set later, just before the Escape, so the
# first drain AFTER the menu-leave save is identifiable in the transcript --
# which is how the IndexedDB read below knows it is worth doing. It is a
# DRAINED signal and not a durability signal (queuePersist's completion
# callback takes no error), so nothing is asserted on it; the durability claim
# is carried by [SETIDB], which reads the bytes back.
eval:(()=>{const W={n:0,tag:''};window.__pw=W;Module.IDBFS.onAutoPersistStateChanged=(a)=>{if(a){W.n++;console.log('[PERSISTSYNC] start '+W.n)}else{console.log('[PERSISTSYNC] idle after '+W.n+(W.tag?' '+W.tag:''))}};return 'watcher armed'})()

click:#start
wait:9000
shot:01-boot1-language-menu

# Enter picks the highlighted language AND leaves the language menu, so this
# is already a menu exit and already a save -- the first [PERSISTSAVE] line of
# the run. It is not the one the gate is about; it is here because the First
# Setup menu is on the other side of it.
key:Enter
until:1:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:2500
shot:02-boot1-first-setup

# BASELINE. user.cfg exists (the boot save wrote it) and PLAYER_1 is still the
# default that Emscripten's USER environment variable produces, "web_user".
eval:window.__set.fs('boot1-first-setup-open')

key:Down
wait:1000
shot:03-boot1-cursor-on-name

# The player edit: five characters typed into the Name field, turning
# "web_user" into "web_userqzxjv". A fixed string rather than a nonce because
# a steps file is static -- the freshness it needs comes from the fresh
# profile, and from the baseline read above showing the file does not contain
# it yet.
key:Q
key:Z
key:X
key:J
key:V
wait:1500
shot:04-boot1-name-typed

# THE POINT OF THIS READ. The screenshot above shows the new name on screen;
# this shows the FILE still says web_user. The edit is live in the game's
# memory and nowhere else, which is precisely the volatility this task exists
# to remove -- and it is what makes the save observed below attributable to
# leaving the menu rather than to typing.
eval:window.__set.fs('boot1-typed-not-yet-left')

eval:(()=>{window.__pw.tag='DRAINED-AFTER-MENU-LEAVE';return 'tag armed'})()
mark:ESCAPE-LEAVES-THE-SETTINGS-MENU
key:Escape
until:2:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
mark:MENU-LEFT
wait:1500
shot:05-boot1-after-escape

eval:window.__set.fs('boot1-after-menu-leave')

# Wait for libidbfs.js to report the write-back queue drained before opening a
# second connection to the same database.
until:1:30000:DRAINED-AFTER-MENU-LEAVE

# THE DURABILITY READ, AND ITS TIMING IS THE WHOLE POINT: this happens while
# the page is still alive and before any unload event of any kind. Whatever it
# finds was put there by the game, from inside its own loop.
#
# WHY THE PROBE NEVER CREATES THE DATABASE. indexedDB.open() creates an empty
# database as a side effect if none exists, and an empty '/persist' with no
# FILE_DATA object store is worse than no database: libidbfs.js's getDB() would
# then open it with no upgrade event and every later transaction would throw.
# So it aborts the upgrade transaction and reports absent:true instead.
# Version 21 is libidbfs.js's own DB_VERSION; opening with a lower one fails.
eval:(async()=>{const P='boot1-idb';const R=await new Promise((res)=>{const r=indexedDB.open('/persist',21);r.onupgradeneeded=(e)=>{try{e.target.transaction.abort()}catch(_){}res({db:null,absent:true})};r.onsuccess=()=>res({db:r.result,absent:false});r.onerror=()=>res({db:null,absent:true,err:String(r.error)});r.onblocked=()=>res({db:null,absent:false,err:'blocked'})});let txt=null,keyCount=null,stores=null;if(R.db){stores=[...R.db.objectStoreNames];if(stores.includes('FILE_DATA')){const o=R.db.transaction(['FILE_DATA'],'readonly').objectStore('FILE_DATA');keyCount=await new Promise((s,j)=>{const q=o.getAllKeys();q.onsuccess=()=>s(q.result.length);q.onerror=()=>j(q.error)});const rec=await new Promise((s,j)=>{const q=o.get('/persist/var/user.cfg');q.onsuccess=()=>s(q.result);q.onerror=()=>j(q.error)});if(rec&&rec.contents)txt=new TextDecoder().decode(rec.contents)}R.db.close()}const pick=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>l.trim().indexOf(k+' ')===0);return m?m.trim().slice(k.length+1).trim():null};console.log('[SETIDB] '+JSON.stringify({phase:P,absent:!!R.absent,open_error:R.err||null,stores:stores,key_count:keyCount,user_cfg_present:txt!==null,bytes:txt===null?null:txt.length,player_1:pick(txt,'PLAYER_1'),first_use:pick(txt,'FIRST_USE')}));return 'idb read'})()

# ===========================================================================
# THE RELOAD. Everything after this mark is a different page load: a new
# Module, a new MEMFS, a new mount, a new populate.
# ===========================================================================
mark:RELOAD-REQUESTED
eval:location.reload()

until:2:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
mark:BOOT-2-POPULATED
wait:1500
shot:06-boot2-ready

eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.h=(s)=>{if(s===null)return null;let x=5381;for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++)x=((x*33)^s.charCodeAt(i))>>>0;return x.toString(16)};S.pick=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>l.trim().indexOf(k+' ')===0);return m?m.trim().slice(k.length+1).trim():null};S.fs=(phase)=>{const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');console.log('[SETFS] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,present:c!==null,bytes:c===null?null:c.length,hash:S.h(c),player_1:S.pick(c,'PLAYER_1'),first_use:S.pick(c,'FIRST_USE'),clobbered:c!==null&&c.indexOf('CLOBBERED-BY-THE-GATE')>=0}));return phase};window.__set=S;return 'helpers installed'})()

# The setting, in a page that has written nothing.
#
# ONLY player_1 IS ASSERTED HERE, NEVER bytes/hash/first_use, AND THAT IS
# DELIBERATE -- see "WHICH SCREEN BOOT 2 LANDS ON" in the header. The file
# boot 2 reads may have been written by boot 1's menu-leave save or by boot
# 1's beforeunload backstop, and those two differ in FIRST_USE and in whether
# the keyboard template is in the file. The player's name is in both, which is
# exactly why it is the thing this gate is about.
eval:window.__set.fs('boot2-before-play')

click:#start
wait:9000

# Whatever the first screen is -- see the header. Nothing is asserted on it.
shot:07-boot2-first-screen
eval:window.__set.fs('boot2-after-boot')

# THE CLOBBER. Overwrite the file with something that is not a config file at
# all, so that what boot 2 writes next can only have come from the running
# program's memory. Without this step, "boot 2's file still has the name"
# would be satisfied by the bytes simply never being touched.
mark:USER-CFG-CLOBBERED-FROM-JS
eval:(()=>{Module.FS.writeFile('/persist/var/user.cfg','CLOBBERED-BY-THE-GATE\n');return 'clobbered'})()
eval:window.__set.fs('boot2-clobbered')

# One Escape, and it is SCREEN-INDEPENDENT ON PURPOSE. uMenu::HandleEvent maps
# SDLK_ESCAPE to uMenu::Exit() for every menu in the game, so this leaves
# whichever menu boot 2 is showing -- the language menu or the main menu --
# and either way that is a menu exit and therefore a save.
#
# WHAT IT DOES *AFTER* THE SAVE DIFFERS, and it is worth knowing before reading
# the screenshots. On the language-menu branch the game simply carries on into
# the rest of the first-use flow. On the MAIN-MENU branch, Escape is Quit:
# gArmagetron.cpp's MainMenu() returns, and the shutdown path that follows
# tears the display down, calls st_SaveConfig() ITSELF -- unconditionally, a
# pre-existing call site -- and reaches SDL_Quit. So screenshots 08 and 09 on
# that branch are a black canvas, and that is the program having exited
# cleanly, not a crash: "SDL_QuitSubSystem called (and ignored)" and "SDL_Quit
# called (and ignored)" are in the transcript immediately after the save.
#
# That shutdown save is also why check S13 below is NOT evidence for this
# task's mechanism, and says so. S12, which counts menu-leave saves between two
# marks, is.
key:Escape
until:3:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
mark:BOOT-2-REWROTE-IT-FROM-MEMORY
wait:1500
shot:08-boot2-after-escape

# THE STRONGEST CLAIM IN THIS FILE. The clobbered file is gone and PLAYER_1 is
# the name again -- written by boot 2's own tConfItemBase::SaveAll from boot
# 2's own memory. Bytes surviving on disk cannot produce this; st_LoadConfig
# has to have parsed the restored value into the running program.
eval:window.__set.fs('boot2-after-menu-leave')
shot:09-boot2-final-frame

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROL: can this transcript see a browser-reported JS error at all? Last,
# because web/shell.html turns an uncaught error into a red failure banner.
# Without it, "no [EXCEPTION] in the run" is a silence, not an observation --
# M1's Firefox transcript was read as clean when it was merely deaf
# (docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md section 9).
mark:positive-control-deliberate-uncaught-error-follows
eval:setTimeout(function(){null.thisIsADeliberateUncaughtError;},0)
wait:2000
shot:10-after-deliberate-uncaught-error
